---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import PersonCard from '../components/PersonCard.astro';
import { team } from '../data/team';

const creators = team.filter((person) => person.org === 'Program Creators');
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Our Story | Taylor the Turtle"
	description="How a classroom observation in 2010 grew into a community-built, provincially recognized child sexual abuse prevention program."
>
	<Header />
	<main id="main" class="paper">
		<div class="container-site py-14 lg:py-20">
			<h1 class="display-tight max-w-[20ch] text-4xl text-balance lg:text-5xl">Our Story</h1>
			<p class="mt-5 max-w-[52ch] text-forest/80">
				How a classroom observation in 2010 grew into a community-built, provincially recognized
				prevention program.
			</p>

			<section id="early-development" class="mt-14 scroll-mt-8" aria-labelledby="early-development-heading">
				<h2 id="early-development-heading" class="display-tight text-3xl text-balance lg:text-4xl">Early development</h2>
				<div class="mt-5 max-w-[65ch] space-y-5 text-forest/85 text-pretty">
					<p>
						Taylor the Turtle was created in 2010 in response to concerns about traditional child
						sexual abuse prevention programs that relied heavily on fear-based messaging and
						abstract “stranger danger” scenarios. While facilitating prevention sessions in an
						elementary school classroom, Program Founder Carrie Sinkowski observed that children
						struggled to understand scenario-based lessons and often became confused, distressed,
						or overly focused on literal details rather than safety concepts.
					</p>
					<p>
						Many commonly used “good touch/bad touch” programs were also found to be triggering
						for survivors, developmentally inappropriate for young children, and ineffective in
						reflecting how abuse most often occurs, typically within trusted relationships rather
						than through strangers.
					</p>
					<p>
						Taylor the Turtle was developed as a community-informed alternative grounded in
						children’s rights, body awareness, emotional literacy, and safety planning. Drawing on
						research, frontline experience with survivors, and feedback from educators, the
						program focuses on strengthening protective factors such as self-esteem, strong adult
						connections, and the ability to recognize and express feelings.
					</p>
					<p>
						Since its creation, Taylor the Turtle has been shaped through ongoing community input
						and has been delivered across schools and community settings. It was later recognized
						by the province as a best practice prevention initiative.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="creators" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-8" aria-labelledby="creators-heading">
				<h2 id="creators-heading" class="display-tight text-3xl text-balance lg:text-4xl">The creators</h2>
				<p class="mt-4 max-w-[60ch] text-forest/80">
					Taylor was brought to life by one family, working alongside their community colleagues.
				</p>
				<div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
					{creators.map((person, index) => <PersonCard person={person} index={index} />)}
				</div>
			</section>
		</div>
	</main>
	<Footer />
</BaseLayout>
